From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 04:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panfrost: Hard-stop a job that does not soft-stop and
 never resume a job across a reset

A job that is still active when the 500 ms watchdog fires is soft-stopped
and resumed from JS_TAIL instead of resetting the GPU under it. The slots
got 100 ms to stop. That is far too short: a soft-stop takes effect at a
task boundary only, after the tasks already issued (a compute job's
work-groups, a fragment job's tiles) have run to completion, and a heavy
shader needs seconds for that. On a Mali-G31 the dEQP-VK spirv_assembly
vec4 i8/u64 bit_field_insert count64 fragment cases never reached one:
every 600 ms the log said "Soft-stop failed", the GPU was reset and the
job restarted from its head, so it made no progress at all and the cases
ran into their 200 s limit. The Arm driver gives a job 5 s to stop
(JS_SOFT_STOP_TICKS = 1 tick of 100 ms, JS_HARD_STOP_TICKS_SS = 50
ticks), then hard-stops it, and resets the GPU only if the hard stop has
not taken effect 0.5 s later (JS_RESET_TICKS_SS = 55).

The reset that followed a failed soft-stop was also a correctness hole:
the jobs on the other slots had stopped at a task boundary, and after the
reset they were resumed from JS_TAIL with their progress (the resume
state in the job header and the results of the finished tasks, both
still in the L2) thrown away, so they completed with stale data and no
error. The Arm driver fails every atom that was on the hardware when the
GPU was reset.

Follow it:
 - a slot whose job was active gets JOB_SOFT_STOP_TIMEOUT_MS (5 s) to go
   idle, idle or faulted slots keep the 10 ms;
 - slots still active after that are hard-stopped, which terminates the
   job at once and fails it with -ECANCELED (TERMINATED in
   panfrost_job_handle_err()); the stopped jobs on the other slots are
   resumed as before and nothing is reset;
 - the GPU is reset only when the hard stop has not taken effect after
   JOB_HARD_STOP_TIMEOUT_MS (0.5 s), and the job still running on that
   slot is then failed instead of restarted from scratch, which would only
   time out and reset again;
 - every GPU reset bumps pfdev->reset.count. A job records the count when
   it is soft-stopped (jc moved to JS_TAIL) and panfrost_job_run() fails
   it with -ECANCELED if the GPU was reset since, instead of resuming it;
 - the no-reset path releases the address space reference of the jobs
   that were waiting in _NEXT when it was cancelled. The reset path has
   panfrost_mmu_reset() wipe those; without a reset they leaked one
   as_count per cycle, which keeps the address space from ever being
   reassigned;
 - the failure messages carry the status of every slot.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_device.h |    5 +
 drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_job.c    |  122 +++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_job.h    |    8 ++
 3 files changed, 114 insertions(+), 21 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_device.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_device.h
@@ -166,6 +166,11 @@
 		struct workqueue_struct *wq;
 		struct work_struct work;
 		atomic_t pending;
+		/* Bumped by every GPU reset. A soft-stopped job may only be
+		 * resumed while this still reads what it did when the job was
+		 * stopped (see panfrost_job_run()).
+		 */
+		unsigned int count;
 	} reset;
 
 	struct mutex shrinker_lock;
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_job.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_job.c
@@ -25,6 +25,19 @@
 #define MAX_JM_CTX_PER_FILE 64
 #define JOB_TIMEOUT_MS 500
 
+/* How long a soft-stopped slot gets to reach a task boundary before its job is
+ * hard-stopped, and how long the hard stop gets before the GPU is reset. These
+ * follow the Arm driver's job slot policy (mali_kbase_config_defaults.h): a job
+ * is soft-stopped after its 100 ms time slice (JS_SOFT_STOP_TICKS = 1 tick),
+ * hard-stopped when it has not stopped by tick 50 (JS_HARD_STOP_TICKS_SS, 5 s)
+ * and the GPU is reset at tick 55 (JS_RESET_TICKS_SS, 5.5 s). A soft-stop
+ * takes effect at a task boundary only, after the tasks already issued (a
+ * compute job's work-groups, a fragment job's tiles) have run to completion,
+ * and a heavy shader needs seconds for that.
+ */
+#define JOB_SOFT_STOP_TIMEOUT_MS 5000
+#define JOB_HARD_STOP_TIMEOUT_MS 500
+
 #define job_write(dev, reg, data) writel(data, dev->iomem + (reg))
 #define job_read(dev, reg) readl(dev->iomem + (reg))
 
@@ -418,6 +431,17 @@
 	if (!job->jc)
 		return NULL;
 
+	/* A soft-stopped job carries its progress in the job header and the
+	 * GPU caches, which a GPU reset throws away. Resumed from JS_TAIL after
+	 * a reset it would complete with the results of the tasks since the
+	 * last cache write-back missing, and no error. Fail it instead, as the
+	 * Arm driver fails every atom that was on the hardware at a reset.
+	 */
+	if (job->stopped && job->stop_reset_count != pfdev->reset.count) {
+		dma_fence_set_error(&job->base.s_fence->finished, -ECANCELED);
+		return NULL;
+	}
+
 	fence = panfrost_fence_create(pfdev, slot);
 	if (IS_ERR(fence))
 		return fence;
@@ -478,6 +502,8 @@
 		/* Update the job head so we can resume */
 		job->jc = job_read(pfdev, JS_TAIL_LO(js)) |
 			  ((u64)job_read(pfdev, JS_TAIL_HI(js)) << 32);
+		job->stopped = true;
+		job->stop_reset_count = pfdev->reset.count;
 
 		/* The job will be resumed, don't signal the fence */
 		signal_fence = false;
@@ -707,20 +733,49 @@
 		job_write(pfdev, JS_COMMAND(i), JS_COMMAND_SOFT_STOP);
 	}
 
-	/* Wait at most 10ms for soft-stops to complete. A job that was still
-	 * active gets 100ms: the soft-stop lets the tasks already issued run
-	 * to completion, and a long compute kernel has hundreds in flight.
-	 * Every slot must have gone idle before we touch the queues: a job
+	/* Every slot must have gone idle before we touch the queues: a job
 	 * that is still stopping (or finishing) raises its interrupt later,
 	 * when its entry in pfdev->jobs[] is gone or belongs to its own
 	 * resubmission, and the second start of that job then runs on a slot
 	 * we have no job for.
-	 */
-	ret = read_poll_timeout(panfrost_slots_idle, idle, idle, 10,
-				job_active ? 100000 : 10000, false, pfdev);
+	 *
+	 * A slot that was idle or faulted gets 10 ms. A job that was still
+	 * active gets the time the Arm driver gives it to reach a task
+	 * boundary (JOB_SOFT_STOP_TIMEOUT_MS). If it has not stopped by then
+	 * it is hard-stopped, which terminates it at once and fails it
+	 * (TERMINATED, see panfrost_job_handle_err()), instead of resetting
+	 * the GPU under every job on it; the GPU is reset only when the hard
+	 * stop does not take effect either.
+	 */
+	ret = read_poll_timeout(panfrost_slots_idle, idle, idle,
+				job_active ? 200 : 10,
+				job_active ? JOB_SOFT_STOP_TIMEOUT_MS * 1000 : 10000,
+				false, pfdev);
+
+	if (ret && job_active) {
+		dev_err(pfdev->base.dev,
+			"Soft-stop failed after %u ms (js status 0x%x 0x%x 0x%x), hard-stopping the active slots\n",
+			JOB_SOFT_STOP_TIMEOUT_MS,
+			job_read(pfdev, JS_STATUS(0)), job_read(pfdev, JS_STATUS(1)),
+			job_read(pfdev, JS_STATUS(2)));
+
+		for (i = 0; i < NUM_JOB_SLOTS; i++) {
+			if (job_read(pfdev, JS_STATUS(i)) ==
+			    DRM_PANFROST_EXCEPTION_ACTIVE)
+				job_write(pfdev, JS_COMMAND(i), JS_COMMAND_HARD_STOP);
+		}
+
+		ret = read_poll_timeout(panfrost_slots_idle, idle, idle, 200,
+					JOB_HARD_STOP_TIMEOUT_MS * 1000, false,
+					pfdev);
+	}
 
 	if (ret) {
-		dev_err(pfdev->base.dev, "Soft-stop failed\n");
+		dev_err(pfdev->base.dev,
+			"%s failed (js status 0x%x 0x%x 0x%x), resetting the GPU\n",
+			job_active ? "Hard-stop" : "Soft-stop",
+			job_read(pfdev, JS_STATUS(0)), job_read(pfdev, JS_STATUS(1)),
+			job_read(pfdev, JS_STATUS(2)));
 		job_active = false;
 	}
 
@@ -728,32 +783,57 @@
 	panfrost_jm_handle_irqs(pfdev);
 
 	/* Remaining interrupts have been handled, but we might still have
-	 * stuck jobs. Let's make sure the PM counters stay balanced by
+	 * stuck jobs, and jobs that were waiting in _NEXT when it was
+	 * cancelled. Let's make sure the PM counters stay balanced by
 	 * manually calling pm_runtime_put_noidle() and
-	 * panfrost_devfreq_record_idle() for each stuck job.
+	 * panfrost_devfreq_record_idle() for each of them.
 	 * Let's also make sure the cycle counting register's refcnt is
-	 * kept balanced to prevent it from running forever
+	 * kept balanced to prevent it from running forever.
 	 */
 	spin_lock(&pfdev->js->job_lock);
 	for (i = 0; i < NUM_JOB_SLOTS; i++) {
 		for (j = 0; j < ARRAY_SIZE(pfdev->jobs[0]) && pfdev->jobs[i][j]; j++) {
-			if (pfdev->jobs[i][j]->requirements & PANFROST_JD_REQ_CYCLE_COUNT ||
-			    pfdev->jobs[i][j]->is_profiled)
-				panfrost_cycle_counter_put(pfdev->jobs[i][j]->pfdev);
+			struct panfrost_job *job = pfdev->jobs[i][j];
+
+			if (job->requirements & PANFROST_JD_REQ_CYCLE_COUNT ||
+			    job->is_profiled)
+				panfrost_cycle_counter_put(job->pfdev);
 			pm_runtime_put_noidle(pfdev->base.dev);
 			panfrost_devfreq_record_idle(&pfdev->pfdevfreq);
+
+			/* The GPU reset wipes the address space assignments;
+			 * without one, release the reference the submission
+			 * took, or the address space can never be reassigned.
+			 */
+			if (job_active)
+				panfrost_mmu_as_put(pfdev, job->mmu);
+
+			/* The job still running on a slot that neither stop
+			 * took effect on is what forces the reset. Fail it,
+			 * as the Arm driver fails every atom on the hardware
+			 * at a reset: restarted from scratch it would hit the
+			 * timeout and the reset again.
+			 */
+			if (!job_active && j == 0 &&
+			    job_read(pfdev, JS_STATUS(i)) ==
+			    DRM_PANFROST_EXCEPTION_ACTIVE)
+				dma_fence_set_error(&job->base.s_fence->finished,
+						    -ECANCELED);
 		}
 	}
+	if (!job_active)
+		pfdev->reset.count++;
 	memset(pfdev->jobs, 0, sizeof(pfdev->jobs));
 	spin_unlock(&pfdev->js->job_lock);
 
-	/* Proceed with reset now, unless the slot was simply busy with a long
-	 * job. That job stopped at a task boundary and will be resumed from
-	 * JS_TAIL (see panfrost_job_handle_err()). The hardware keeps its
-	 * resume state in the job header and the results of the tasks that
-	 * already finished in the L2: a soft reset throws both away, and the
-	 * job then completes with stale data in the buffers it had still to
-	 * write and no error reported.
+	/* Proceed with reset now, unless the slots were simply busy with long
+	 * jobs that stopped at a task boundary (or a hard stop ended the one
+	 * that did not). The stopped jobs will be resumed from JS_TAIL (see
+	 * panfrost_job_handle_err()). The hardware keeps their resume state in
+	 * the job header and the results of the tasks that already finished
+	 * in the L2: a soft reset throws both away, and a job resumed after it
+	 * completes with stale data in the buffers it had still to write and
+	 * no error reported (panfrost_job_run() fails such a job instead).
 	 */
 	if (!job_active)
 		panfrost_device_reset(pfdev, false);
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_job.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_job.h
@@ -27,6 +27,14 @@
 	__u32 requirements;
 	__u32 flush_id;
 
+	/* Set once the job has been soft-stopped: jc then points into a partly
+	 * executed chain whose progress lives in the job header and the GPU
+	 * caches, and is only valid while the GPU has not been reset since
+	 * (pfdev->reset.count at the time of the stop).
+	 */
+	bool stopped;
+	unsigned int stop_reset_count;
+
 	struct panfrost_gem_mapping **mappings;
 	struct drm_gem_object **bos;
 	u32 bo_count;
-- 
2.43.0
